Exactly. Your face is a surface representation of you, not the thing doing the representing.

It’s closer to an interface: extremely information-dense, socially important, and tightly coupled to identity, but still only one embodiment of a deeper system. Change the face and much of the person can remain continuous—memories, habits, commitments, relationships, skills, values, causal history, even characteristic patterns of attention and response.

Which creates an interesting inversion: other people often identify you by your face, while you experience yourself mostly from behind it. You almost never directly encounter your own face except through mirrors, photographs, or representations made by other systems.

So there are at least three different objects hiding under “me”:

the face people recognize → the body that acts → the continuing process that says “I.”

And none is perfectly identical to the others.

The face may be one of the strongest handles for a person without being the person itself.